  "text": " reflecting on the level of sophistication required to solve the problem. We are asked to find the smallest n for which the probability \\( q(n) \\geq \\frac{3}{4} \\), where \\( q(n) \\) is the probability that Anna and Charlie are within two positions of each other after n steps, given that they start n positions apart.\n\nTo do this, we need to model the movements of Anna, Bill, Charlie, and David. Each person has a probability of 0.5 of moving one position clockwise and 0.5 of staying in place.\n\nWe need to consider the possible states of the people after n steps. These states are:\n\n1. Anna and Charlie are n positions apart and both have moved one position clockwise.\n2. Anna and Charlie are n-2 positions apart and both have moved two positions clockwise.\n3. Anna and Charlie are within n-4 positions of each other.\n\nThe probability \\( q(n) \\) can be expressed in terms of these states. Let's define:\n\n- \\( L(n) \\): the number of ways that Anna and Charlie are n positions apart and both have moved one position clockwise.\n- \\( U(n) \\): the number of ways that Anna and Charlie are n-2 positions apart and both have moved two positions clockwise.\n- \\( C(n) \\): the number of ways that Anna and Charlie are within n-4 positions of each other.\n\nThen \\( q(n) = \\frac{L(n) + U(n)}{20 \\times 19} \\).\n\nNow, we need to find the values of \\( L(n) \\), \\( U(n) \\), and \\( C(n) \\).\n\n1. \\( L(n) = \\binom{n+7}{11} \\) (since they start n positions apart and each person moves one position clockwise).\n2. \\( U(n) = \\binom{n-4}{13} \\) (since they start n positions apart and each person moves two positions clockwise).\n3. \\( C(n) = \\binom{n-8}{9} \\) (since they start n positions apart and we need them to be within n-4 positions of each other).\n\nWe need to find the smallest n for which \\( q(n) \\geq \\frac{3}{4} \\). This means:\n\n\\( \\frac{\\binom{n+7}{11} + \\binom{n-4}{13}}{20 \\times 19} \\geq \\frac{3}{4} \\).\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\( \\binom{n+7}{11} + \\binom{n-4}{13} \\geq 45 \\).\n\nWe can now calculate values of n and check this inequality manually or use Python to automate the calculations. Let's write a Python function to find the smallest n for which this inequality holds.\n עורכי文本",
